Translated Content:
Soon Aluda cast his eye on the Kists’ trail.  
He shifted swiftly; the gun went off with a crack:  
one thieving Ghilghveli had a bad moment;  
he falls from the horse, hanging head and neck downward;  
the bullet wound is at the shoulder blade, and fire seems to cling to it;  
the slain Ghilghveli, his companion, is carrying the flintlock.  
Mutsal’s gun burst forth, the cliff face would split apart,  
and the lead, dug out by the shot, would fall into Aluda’s skirt.

II. The sun rose into the sky, the mists descended into the gorges;  
the giants of the Caucasus have gathered at the edge of the sky.  
The hawks have set out for the hunt, the swift destroyers of birds;  
the falcons are chasing the eagles, their companions in eating for free.

I could not bring myself to cut off my right hand;  
my heart grew angry with me: “Do not do what is to be done with difficulty:  
‘Let the name suffer loss; I am preferable to you as a counselor.’”

III. It grew dark, the waters are weeping, the hem of night burns us;  
It is the time of the stars’ twinkling, of dew falling upon the grass,  
of the dead souls rising from the grave, the time of their quiet song.

Khetelauri grew furious, taking on the color of a wolf;  
he drew his French sword, and the light burst forth like the sun.  
He struck the bull on the neck, and its head rolled down below.  
Then he pleaded with his lord: “Do not spare my son for me;  
let his belly be given to ruin, that unbaptized hero.”

At hearing this, Mindia’s eyes welled with tears;  
he can no longer help Aluda.  
He crosses his arms over his chest in grief;  
the sons of the Khevsurs, enraged, raise their shields.  
They must make Ketelauri know it;  
the bronze rings ring out sharply.  
The Khevsurs, seated in a host, are overtaken by the boys,  
one of them has seized the bones of his right hand.

They took the guest’s armor away and invited him inside;  
the woman followed behind, and Jokhola went ahead;  
Zviadauri followed along too, as a sworn brother was made.

The sun had not yet risen, the dew still slept on the grass,  
the breeze had not yet blown, had not yet spread below,  
when countless men and women gathered over the meadow.  
They were bringing Zviadauri, hands bound, into the crowd;  
everyone rejoiced at his death—who would begin the mourning?!  

Death frightens us all; if it is another who is being killed, we want to watch;  
men often do not feel how great a sin they are committing.  
How many evil souls I know who walk with untroubled brows;  
and who would not desire the destruction of the one who torments him?!

The evening hour has come; from the mountain the sunbeam has descended,  
darkness is creeping in, and the soul has departed from the light.  
The sunbeams have stood still, have all stood still; the sand no longer glitters;  
nowhere is seen, has vanished, the white hair of the black mountain;  
the face of that rocky cliff, embroidered and woven with sorrow,  
which is always mourning, from which clear tears flow.  
Mourning suits death, a dead brother — a sister’s weeping;  
the forest — the flight of ox and deer, and at times — the wolf’s howl.  
For a brave man — death in battle, in his hand — a broken sword;  
for war — the joy of the victors and the defeat of the enemy.

They were met by the Kists’ army too, hidden along the riverbank;  
they fired their guns, with their carbines belted tight;  
the Khevsur boys, all bristling, would not give way on the road.  

From both sides they shoot, shouting, with their guns;  
both sides are troubled by the matter, and both fight steadfastly.  
The bullets drank plenty of blood — Kutchia mowed them down —  
yet the Kists still did not break, standing like a fortress tower.  

Baburauli advised the Khevsurs to go down sword against sword.  
So it came to swords, and the shield gave way for the cutting path;  
the Khevsurs have a precious treasure and spoil to defend.  
Pharo, do not deceive Gordas; you are his servant.  
In front of the Khevsurs running downhill, a cliff-like precipice appeared.

The Kist host fled, rushing toward the towers with a roar;  
the Khevsurs pursued them, with their helmets gleaming overhead;  
they gathered Zviada’s bones in a whirl on the cemetery:  
some lay here, some there, torn apart by birds, scattered in the air.

The wind blows, and below, through the gorges, it howls among the mountains;  
a woman stands, gazing at the river, on a cliff, with her hair spread loose;  
it faintly reminds me of a dim, fading star, with trembling lips.  
She does not speak... trembling, she quivers as she looks at the river;  
how dreadfully it roars, how dreadfully it rushes on!  
The black gorge, with bared teeth, fierce and savage, rages,  
“Do not kill yourself!” — if only someone would advise her, at least.  
The woman closed her eyes and swiftly plunged into the whirlpool.

I. There were Khevsurs at the inn, and beer was boiling in the *tsika*;  
they sat on the *ban* and feasted, drinking it from cups;  
some played the *panduri*, singing loudly over it,  
and to the listeners’ hearts they brought heroic tales as a bridge;  
with fervor they counted the names of heroes in verses, as something sacred;  
the old men drew their pipes, and smoke wrapped them like a mist,  
they explained their stories, speaking of the heroes’ glory.  
For the instruction of the young they recalled, with praise:  
“You know, from now on, whoever proves himself a man!”

He learned the wisdom of the demons from that time on, the captive;  
The sky, the earth, and the forest began to converse with him.  
Only evil could not take root in his heart;  
All the rest of the demon-kind’s tricks he learned as skill.  
He fears nothing anymore, even if lightning were to strike him.  
The demons are enraged when they see it — the peasant has become enlightened.  
Captivity no longer frightens him, nor do demonic schemes;  
Today or tomorrow, he hopes, he will give the demons a beating.

Swift as a bullet, cunning as a serpent,  
all of Pshav-Khevsureti reveres him and finds comfort in him;  
Crowned Tamar is forever saying this:  
“Only let Mindia stay with me, and with him the mountain folk are a people;  
however hard the enemy may try, he can do me no harm.”

He knows the stratagems that defeat and crush the foe;  
if he reaches the wounded while they are still alive, there is no need to fear death.  
His incomparable remedy heals even one cut in half;  
thus he never dwindles in war, for he always has a great army;  
far and wide has his tale of adventure gone forth.

A tower is attached to the house, blackened with soot,  
day and night shaken by the firing of rifles.  
What will give the poor soul rest,  
while enemies are all around,  
while the desire for revenge has lodged in the hearts of humankind,  
and many bullets fired by the enemy have struck his chest!
